      "message": "I have been thinking more carefully about the overall permission management mechanism, particularly the process of granting, revoking, and modifying permissions across different peers.\n\nIn this context, I would like to raise a number of additional questions and scenarios that I think should be considered and clarified, both in relation to this particular patch and independently of it, at the architectural level.\n\n1. Client crashes, hangs, and network interruptions\n\nWhat happens if, before, during, or after a permission change, one of the involved clients suddenly crashes, becomes unresponsive, or loses its connection?\n\nThis applies both to administrators initiating or approving permission changes and to peers whose permissions are being granted, revoked, or modified.\n\nRelevant scenarios include:\n\nA client switches from mobile data to Wi-Fi, resulting in a temporary network interruption.\nOne or more peers go offline, either temporarily or permanently.\nA network connection is interrupted during the transmission, validation, or application of a permission-related commit.\nA peer blocks an administrator or is removed from the conversation while a permission change or voting process is in progress.\n\n2. Persistence and recovery\n\nAre pending votes and already submitted approvals persisted reliably, so that they survive client crashes, restarts, or extended offline periods?\n\nCan interrupted permission changes be safely resumed or recovered when the affected peers reconnect?\n\n3. Commit ordering, duplication, and consistency\n\nHow does the system handle delayed, duplicated, or out-of-order commits?\n\nIs it guaranteed that the same permission change cannot be applied multiple times or that an outdated commit cannot overwrite a more recent permission state?\n\n4. Network partitions and conflicting decisions\n\nWhat happens if the peers become temporarily separated into different network partitions and cannot communicate with each other?\n\nCould different groups of peers temporarily accept conflicting permission changes or reach different conclusions about the current roles and approval threshold?\n\nHow does the system ensure that all peers eventually converge on one consistent and valid permission state?\n\n5. Simultaneous and overlapping voting processes\n\nHow are concurrent permission changes handled?\n\nFor example, what happens if two administrators initiate different role changes at approximately the same time, or if a change to the approval threshold is being voted on while another permission change is already in progress?\n\nCould these operations interfere with each other or result in an inconsistent state?\n\n6. Administrators leaving or being removed\n\nWhat happens to pending votes and ongoing approval processes if an administrator leaves the conversation or is removed while a vote is in progress?\n\nAre their previously submitted votes still valid, or are they discarded?\n\n7. Preventing a conversation from losing all administrators\n\nIs it technically guaranteed that a conversation can never end up without an administrator, including in situations involving simultaneous demotions, removals, or other concurrent operations?\n\n8. Stale permissions and authorization\n\nHow does the system handle commits submitted by an administrator whose privileges have changed or been revoked in the meantime?\n\nFor example, an administrator might initiate a permission change while still holding administrative privileges, but lose those privileges before the corresponding commit is processed.\n\nAt what point is authorization checked, and which permission state is considered authoritative?\n\n9. Peers rejoining a conversation\n\nHow is it ensured that a peer returning after an extended offline period receives the current and authoritative permission state?\n\nCould outdated local state, previously held privileges, or old pending votes accidentally be restored or applied again?\n\n10. Interaction with Jami\u0027s global blocking mechanism\n\nAnother aspect worth clarifying is the relationship between global user blocking and conversation-level administration.\n\nAs far as I understand, a user can block another user globally, whereas administrators manage membership and permissions within a conversation.\n\nCould a conversation member therefore block an administrator, and if so, what consequences would this have for ongoing or future permission-related operations and voting?\n\nFor example, can a blocked administrator still participate in voting, and what happens to votes already submitted?\n\nMore generally, I think it would be important to ensure that crashes, network interruptions, offline peers, concurrent operations, blocking, or removal cannot leave a conversation in an inconsistent or partially updated state, or cause permission changes and voting processes to become permanently stuck.\n\nI am not necessarily suggesting that every possible failure scenario needs to be handled individually in this particular patch. However, I believe these cases should be explicitly considered and addressed as part of the overall permission management architecture, wherever technically possible.",
